 "intro_html": "Use <b>regular oyster mushrooms</b>, with broad grey or tan fan-shaped caps and short side stems. Tear them into small, flat pieces so the frilly edges crisp while the mushroom stays juicy. For seafood, see <a href=\"jumbo-shrimp-tempura.html\">jumbo shrimp tempura</a>.",
 "mise": [],
 "more_label": "More japanese:",
 "next": {
  "slug": "oyakodon",
  "title": "Oyakodon"
 },
 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Shiitake caps and small maitake clusters also work.</li><li>Keep pieces small and flat enough to cook through while the coating stays pale.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Other mushrooms"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Use kombu-shiitake dashi instead of bonito dashi.</li><li>The batter contains egg; this version is vegetarian with vegetable dashi, not vegan.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Vegetarian dipping sauce"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Try sweet potato or lotus root in <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"len\" data-u=\"mm\" data-a=\"5\" data-b=\"10\" data-s=\"0\">5–10 mm</span> slices; kabocha in thin wedges; aubergine in thin fans; asparagus spears; shiitake caps; small maitake clusters; or fresh shiso leaves, battered on one side.</li><li>Dry everything first.</li><li>Roots need longer than leaves: fry separately and test the centre for tenderness.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Other good tempura choices"
  }
 ],
